+		<Tag>TXT_KEY_ADVCIV_SAS_CORE_AI_PERSONALITY_AGGREGATES_PEDIA_SR</Tag>
+		<English>[H1]AI Personality Aggregates (AdvCiv-SAS)[\H1]Note: Doc written by ChatGPT (and full AI Aggregates code also comes from ChatGPT, i only implemented it (copy paste) after prompting it (and did the table and such in python with ChatGPT's help but anyways)). As for an example to see it, see [LINK=LEADER_GANDHI]Gandhi's leader page for example[\LINK].[PARAGRAPH:2]AI Personality Aggregates are a unique feature of AdvCiv-SAS that provide insight into how each leader behaves, beyond visible traits like Aggressive or Creative. They are based on hidden AI values found in CIV4LeaderHeadInfos.xml, such as MaxWarRand, BuildUnitProb, PeaceWeight, and many others. These values are normally invisible during gameplay but strongly influence how each AI leader acts in diplomacy, war, trade, and empire management.[PARAGRAPH:1]Aggregates are computed by combining multiple of these hidden values into a single number between 0-100. This number is normalized relative to all other leaders using a min-max formula. For example, if the lowest value among all leaders is 10 and the highest is 50, then a leader with 30 would score 50 on the normalized scale. The point is to allow you to compare leaders easily on a single axis like "Aggressiveness" or "Builder Tendency".[PARAGRAPH:1]Each behavior group - called an aggregate - consists of a name, a score (0-100), and a visual scale made of symbols such as ### or >>>>>. The score reflects the leader's strength in that area, while the symbol makes it easy to understand at a glance. The symbols vary depending on the type of trait: # for war traits, > for builder or efficiency traits, = or : for diplomatic traits.[PARAGRAPH:1]Let us walk through one example. The "Sneaky" trait is calculated using three XML values: getDemandRebukedSneakProb, getEspionageWeight, and getDogpileWarRand. For each of these, the AI has a number in the XML - for instance, getEspionageWeight might be 40. We collect the values for all leaders, then normalize the selected leader's value to a number between 0-100. If one of the XML values is inverted (meaning a lower number makes the AI more inclined to do something, like lower WonderConstructRand means more wonder-building), then the normalization is reversed for that value before averaging.[PARAGRAPH:1]Then we average the three normalized values, and the result is the final aggregate score. So if a leader has a sneak prob of 80/100, espionage of 60/100, and dogpile tendency of 40/100, the result would be 60/100 on "Sneaky". That is what gets displayed in the AI Personality panel.[PARAGRAPH:1]These aggregates are shown on the right side of the Sevopedia Leader page. Each entry shows the name of the trait, the computed score, and the visual scale. The traits are grouped into categories like War Strategy, Diplomacy, Economic Preferences, Victory Strategy, Attitude Modifiers, Trade Thresholds, and Aggregates.[PARAGRAPH:1]Aggregates are new, and they are not part of standard Civilization IV or AdvCiv. They were designed fully by ChatGPT, including the Python logic and the display strategy. All the math is done using Python inside SevoPediaLeader.py, and the values are pulled live from the XML data already defined by the game. That means they work for all existing leaders and any custom leaders you add - as long as those leaders have AI values, the system will work for them too.[PARAGRAPH:1]As a modder or player, you can use this information to understand the AI better. If an AI has high "Stubborn", you know that even at Friendly they may refuse your deals. If another has high "Dogpiler", be careful when you are weak in a war - they might declare on you opportunistically. Traits like "Builder", "Flexible", or "Tech Hoarder" give you clues about their priorities.[PARAGRAPH:1]This system does not override anything in the game - it simply makes visible what was already there but hidden. It helps with both immersion and gameplay understanding. The traits are not made up: they are just summaries of existing AI behavior, now available in a readable form.[PARAGRAPH:1]You can edit the behavior formulas directly in Python. Every trait is defined as a list of XML getters, each with an optional inverse flag. Modders can create new traits or adjust the weighting as they see fit. The layout is customizable too, but even without edits, the system is plug-and-play.[PARAGRAPH:1]In conclusion, AI Personality Aggregates are a powerful addition for any player or modder who wants to understand the AI beyond simple trait labels. They show you how an AI is wired, what it values, and how it is likely to behave in a variety of situations. For learning, planning, diplomacy, or modding - they are an invaluable tool.</English>
+	</TEXT>
